                  <!-- The 4 Invariant Observability Planes -->
                  <div style="font-weight: 700; color: #ffffff; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 0.65rem;">
                    📊 The 4 Invariant Observability Planes &amp; Mathematical Thresholds
                  </div>
                  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                    <!-- Plane 1 -->
                    <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem;">
                      <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 700; color: #38bdf8; font-family: var(--font-mono); margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                        1. Work Process &amp; Dispatcher Plane
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.74rem; color: #94a3b8; line-height: 1.45; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        ABAP Dispatcher &amp; Concurrent Manager process allocation. If DIA processes reach 100%, user transactions queue in dispatcher memory.
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: #69f0ae; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 4px;">
                        Formula: W_sat = (DIA_act + UPD_act) / Total_WP * 100<br>
                        Sweet Spot: Warn &gt; 80.0% &bull; Crit &gt; 92.0%
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Plane 2 -->
                    <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem;">
                      <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 700; color: #fbbf24; font-family: var(--font-mono); margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                        2. Integration &amp; RPC Queue Plane
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.74rem; color: #94a3b8; line-height: 1.45; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        RFC Gateway (SM58) &amp; IDoc Status 51 (Application Document Not Posted) and Status 64 backlogs indicate external partner or commit deadlocks.
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: #69f0ae; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 4px;">
                        Formula: B_int = Sum(Unprocessed_Queue) + Status_51_Backlog<br>
                        Sweet Spot: Warn &ge; 25 rec &bull; Crit &ge; 100 rec
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Plane 3 -->
                    <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem;">
                      <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 700; color: #fb7185; font-family: var(--font-mono); margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                        3. Concurrency &amp; Enqueue Lock Plane
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.74rem; color: #94a3b8; line-height: 1.45; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        Central Services Enqueue lock table (SM12 / ERS). Lock overflow causes catastrophic user dumps with SYSTEM_NO_MORE_PAGING.
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: #69f0ae; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 4px;">
                        Formula: L_util = Active_Locks / Max_Capacity * 100<br>
                        Sweet Spot: Warn &gt; 70.0% &bull; Crit &gt; 85.0%
                      </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Plane 4 -->
                    <div style="background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem;">
                      <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 700; color: #34d399; font-family: var(--font-mono); margin-bottom: 0.35rem;">
                        4. User Experience &amp; Dialog Latency
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.74rem; color: #94a3b8; line-height: 1.45; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                        Dialog / Transaction Mean Response Time across WebGUI and Fiori (T_CPU + T_DB + T_Wait + T_RollIn). Over 1000ms causes severe operational drag.
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.72rem; font-family: var(--font-mono); color: #69f0ae; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 4px;">
                        Formula: T_resp = T_CPU + T_DB + T_Wait + T_RollIn<br>
                        Sweet Spot: Warn &gt; 1000ms &bull; Crit &gt; 2500ms
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <!-- Zero-WAN Air-Gap Collection Mechanics -->
                  <div style="background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 8px; padding: 1.25rem;">
                    <div style="font-weight: 700; color: #ffffff; font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                      🛡️ In-Perimeter Zero-WAN Security &amp; Air-Gap Collection Guarantee
                    </div>
                    <p style="font-size: 0.82rem; color: #94a3b8; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 0.75rem 0;">
                      Enterprise ERP telemetry is sampled via local in-perimeter loopback mechanisms:
                    </p>
                    <ul style="font-size: 0.8rem; color: #cbd5e1; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem;">
                      <li><strong>SAP Control SOAP Daemon (Port 50013):</strong> Authenticated via local Unix domain sockets or loopback HTTP. Queries <code>GetProcessList</code> and <code>EnqGetStatistic</code> with zero kernel impact and zero binary agent installations.</li>
                      <li><strong>Zero Egress of Financial / PII Data:</strong> Telemetry collection is restricted to pure SRE operational invariants (queue lengths, milliseconds, lock counts). User payloads, invoice figures, and employee records are strictly excluded from metric streams.</li>
                      <li><strong>Hardware Keyring Isolation:</strong> SaaS API tokens (Salesforce, Workday, ServiceNow) are stored in local AES-256-GCM hardware keyrings with zero external cloud sync.</li>
                    </ul>
                  </div>
                </div>


                <!-- 🏢 Section 8.9: Enterprise Packaging & Strategic Demarcation: The Clear Line of Demarcation -->
                <div id="admin-strategic-demarcation" style="margin-bottom: 3rem; background: linear-gradient(145deg, rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.96), rgba(11, 17, 32, 0.94)); border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.45); border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem; box-shadow: 0 16px 45px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">
                  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); padding-bottom: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px;">
                    <div>
                      <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 6px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
                        <span style="font-size: 1.6rem;">🏢</span>
                        <h3 style="font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 800; color: #ffffff; margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono);">
                          Section 8.9 &bull; Enterprise Packaging &amp; Strategic Demarcation: The Clear Line of Demarcation
                        </h3>
                      </div>
                      <div style="font-size: 0.8rem; color: #38bdf8; font-family: var(--font-mono);">
                        SPEC: VA-SPEC-DEMARCATION-001 &bull; Day-0/Day-1 Active Project Engine vs. Day-2 Permanent Operational Backbone &bull; Non-Public Strategy
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
                      <span class="status-pill healthy" style="font-size: 0.72rem; padding: 4px 10px;">LIFECYCLE ALIGNED</span>
                      <a href="/docs/runbooks/RUNBOOK-PRODUCT-DEMARCATION-MIGRATION-SUITE-VS-FULLSTACK-SUITE.html" target="_blank" style="padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 6px; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.15); border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.35); color: #38bdf8; text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.76rem; font-weight: 700;">
                        📖 SRE Runbook ↗
                      </a>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <p style="font-size: 0.88rem; color: #cbd5e1; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                    Eliminating the procurement <strong>&quot;Version Trap&quot;</strong> and <strong>&quot;Migration Churn Trap.&quot;</strong>
                    Transitioning from generic &quot;Edition&quot; labels to <strong><span class="dcouplr-wordmark"><span class="dcouplr-bracket">&lt;</span><span class="dcouplr-text">dcouplr</span><span class="dcouplr-bracket">/&gt;</span></span> Migration Suite</strong> and <strong><span class="dcouplr-wordmark"><span class="dcouplr-bracket">&lt;</span><span class="dcouplr-text">dcouplr</span><span class="dcouplr-bracket">/&gt;</span></span> Full-Stack Suite</strong> creates an unmistakable operational line of demarcation, aligning product packaging directly with the enterprise buyer's infrastructure lifecycle:
                  </p>

                  <!-- The Version Trap & Migration Churn Trap Callout Card -->
                  <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7), rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95)); border: 1px solid rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.3); border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
                    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                      <span style="font-size: 1.2rem;">⚠️</span>
                      <h4 style="font-size: 0.98rem; font-weight: 800; color: #fbbf24; font-family: var(--font-mono); margin: 0; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.03em;">
                        Why We Eliminated &quot;Edition 1&quot; &amp; &quot;Edition 2&quot; Ambiguity
                      </h4>
                    </div>
                    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 1.25rem; font-size: 0.81rem; color: #cbd5e1; line-height: 1.55;">
                      <div>
                        <strong style="color: #fff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">1. The &quot;Version Trap&quot;</strong>
                        In enterprise procurement, &quot;Edition 1&quot; falsely sounds like Version 1.0 (outdated or soon-to-be-deprecated). Buyers ask: <em>&quot;Why buy Edition 1 if Edition 2 is already out?&quot;</em> In reality, both suites share the identical modern engine, but serve fundamentally different stages of your infrastructure lifecycle.
                      </div>
                      <div>
                        <strong style="color: #fff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">2. Obscured Value Proposition</strong>
                        Generic &quot;Edition&quot; numbers reveal nothing about the actual business problem solved—whether you are executing high-velocity IaC/data refactoring or deploying permanent 24x7 in-VPC telemetry.
                      </div>
                      <div>
                        <strong style="color: #fff; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;">3. The &quot;Migration Churn Trap&quot;</strong>
                        If buyers perceive <span class="dcouplr-wordmark"><span class="dcouplr-bracket">&lt;</span><span class="dcouplr-text">dcouplr</span><span class="dcouplr-bracket">/&gt;</span></span> solely as a transient migration utility, they treat it as temporary CapEx and churn post-cutover. Demarcating the permanent Full-Stack Suite guarantees long-term operational SLA defense.
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>

